= Erim Between İnönü and the Generals

The investigation requested by Nihat Erim has concluded, but the results are far from what İnönü had hoped for. Ministry of National Defense, led by Ferit Melen, falls under incredible pressure from the entire military apparatus regarding the investigation, resulting in an unfair and biased report being sent to Erim who then has to publicize it. Reports claims that the military prosecutors did not exceed their authority and that the accusations were not aimed at swaying public opinion against the CHP. In response to this explanation, İnönü becomes enraged at this conclusion, unable to believe that such serious accusations could have been made against him and the CHP without righteous persecution. He again asserts that the military prosecutors must be dismissed from their posts in order to avoid setting a dangerous precedent for military interference in civilian affairs.

Within the top echelons of the Turkish Armed Forces, there is division. Muhsin Batur, the Air Force Commander shifts his supports to İnönü. He believes that the accusations leveled against such an important figure in the founding of the Republic were far too severe to have been made by military prosecutors. Desperate to resolve the problem, Erim approaches the generals of 12th March He reminds them of İnönü's brilliant contributions to the country, as well as his support for Parliament despite the March 12 Memorandum. He pleads with them to recognize the injustice that has been done to İnönü and encourage the military authorities to offer him an apology, in the hope that the situation may be resolved.

Despite Erim's attempts to resolve the issue, his efforts largely fail. Memduh Tağmaç (Chief of the General Staff), Faruk Gürler (Army Commander), and Celal Eyiceoğlu (Navy Commander) refuse to take action against the prosecutors. They state that the military judiciary acted within its authority. Though Muhsin Batur feels sympathy for İnönü, in the end, all members of the Big Four support the Ankara Martial Law Court. Therefore, Erim's demands for intervention go unheeded. In this situation, it appears increasingly difficult for Erim to satisfy İnönü without angering the very generals on whom his government depends.

